293 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 133 0 R
/E (This paper explores the architectural advantages of silicon photonics, including bandwidth density, reduced energy consumption, and resilience to interference. It also examines the major challenges hindering widespread adoption, such as manufacturing complexity and the lack of standardization across the industry [2][5]. Furthermore, it evaluates the evolving AI-driven landscape in 2026, where photonic interconnects are rapidly transitioning from experimental technologies to essential components in high-performance computing systems [4][6])
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
294 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 133 0 R
/T (INTRODUCTION)
/E (INTRODUCTION)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
295 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 133 0 R
/E (For decades, the computing industry has relied on transistor scaling to drive performance improvements. Increasing transistor density enabled faster processors and more powerful systems, forming the foundation of exponential growth in computational capabilities. However, while computational units have advanced significantly, the communication infrastructure connecting them has not kept pace. This imbalance has resulted in what is widely referred to as the interconnect bottleneck, a critical limitation that restricts overall system performance. Prior research indicates that as computational systems scale, inefficiencies in data movement and parallel execution increasingly dominate performance and energy costs [9][10].)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
296 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 133 0 R
/E (In modern computing environments, particularly in data centers and AI clusters, the need for efficient data movement has become as important as computation itself. Large-scale AI models require massive parallel processing across distributed GPU clusters, where thousands of processing units must exchange data continuously. Traditional copper-based interconnects struggle in this context due to signal degradation, electromagnetic interference, and increasing power consumption over longer distances [7]. These inefficiencies not only limit performance but also significantly increase operational costs in large-scale deployments. In highly parallel intelligent systems, )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
