320 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 1198 0 R
/T (Manufacturing Complexity and Material Constraints)
/E (Manufacturing Complexity and Material Constraints)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
321 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 135 0 R
/E (Despite their advantages, Photonic Integrated Circuits face significant challenges in manufacturing and scalability. While silicon photonics benefits from compatibility with established CMOS fabrication processes, one of the most critical limitations lies in efficient on-chip light generation. Silicon, by its physical nature, is an indirect bandgap material and therefore an inefficient emitter of light.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
322 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 135 0 R
/E (To address this, manufacturers must integrate external materials such as indium phosphide \(InP\) or other III-V semiconductors, which are capable of efficient light emission. However, this hybrid integration introduces substantial complexity into the fabrication process. Aligning different material systems with high precision increases production costs and can negatively impact yield, making large-scale manufacturing more difficult [2][3].)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
323 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 1199 0 R
/T (Design Ecosystem and Lack of Standardization)
/E (Design Ecosystem and Lack of Standardization)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
324 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 135 0 R
/E (Another major barrier to widespread adoption is the relative immaturity of the photonics design ecosystem. Unlike traditional electronic design, which benefits from decades of standardization, robust toolchains, and well-established fabrication workflows, photonic design is still evolving.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
325 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 135 0 R
/E (Currently, there is a lack of standardized design libraries, simulation tools, packaging techniques, and testing methodologies. This forces companies and research institutions to develop custom solutions tailored to their specific implementations. Such fragmentation slows innovation and increases development costs, as interoperability between different systems becomes limited [5][8].)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
326 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 135 0 R
/T (CHALLENGES AND LIMITATIONS)
/E (CHALLENGES AND LIMITATIONS)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
327 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 135 0 R
/E (Efforts by industry organizations to establish common standards are underway, but widespread adoption will require coordinated collaboration across multiple stakeholders, including semiconductor manufacturers, system designers, and software tool providers.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
