388 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 138 0 R
/E (Why Deep Quantum Circuits Forget Their Own History - And Why That's a Crisis)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
389 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 138 0 R
/E (Abstract)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
390 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 138 0 R
/E (Variation quantum algorithms \(VQAs\) are widely seen as the most viable path toward quantum advantage, yet they face a fundamental scalability crisis known as the barren plateau. As quantum circuits grow in depth to handle complex tasks, they increasingly suffer from noise-induced information loss, effectively "forgetting" their historical parameter settings. This article explores how hardware noise, architectural connectivity, and algebraic structures converge to flatten the optimization landscape. By examining the roles of contractive maps, the linear depth barrier, and the curse of dimensionality, we highlight why deep circuits lose their training signal and how engineered strategies like controlled dissipation are being used to restore circuit memory and algorithmic viability.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
391 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 138 0 R
/E (The Contractive Nature of Noise Maps)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
392 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 138 0 R
/E (The primary reason deep circuits lose their history is the mathematical property of noise channels known as contractivity. In a layered error model, parameterized unitaries are alternated with completely positive trace-preserving \(CPTP\) noise maps. These maps naturally make quantum states more indistinguishable over time, a process quantified by the contraction coefficient or Lipschitz constant of the map. For strictly contractive noise, such as amplitude damping or certain Pauli channels, repeated applications drive the system toward a unique fixed point regardless of the initial parameters chosen. Consequently, as the total number of layers in a circuit increases, the system exponentially forgets its dependency on historical parameters, rendering further depth useless for training. \(Schumann et al., 2024\))
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
393 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 138 0 R
/E (Hardware Architecture as a Scaffold for Noise propagation)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
394 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 138 0 R
/E (The loss of information is not uniform across all devices; rather, it is heavily influenced by the connectivity architecture of the processor. Quantum architectures can be represented as connected graphs where nodes are qubits and edges represent two-qubit gate pathways \(Goyal et al., 2024\). Research using machine learning shows that superconducting systems are highly sensitive to global connectivity measures like betweenness centrality and spectral entropy. )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
395 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 138 0 R
/E (INTRODUCTION)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
396 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 138 0 R
/E (The Noisy Intermediate-Scale Quantum \(NISQ\) era is defined by devices that possess significant qubit counts but remain limited by high error rates. Variation quantum circuits serve as a bridge in this landscape, employing classical optimizers to train quantum systems for machine learning and chemistry. However, as these circuits become deeper, they encounter "noise-induced barren plateaus" \(NIBPs\), where the gradient of the cost function disappears exponentially. This loss of sensitivity to trainable parameters creates a flat optimization landscape where finding a solution becomes statistically impossible. )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
