/P 139 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
408 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 139 0 R
/E (The Linear Depth Barrier and Pauli Noise)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
409 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 139 0 R
/E (A critical analytical result shows that under local Pauli noise, the training signal vanishes exponentially if the circuit depth grows linearly with the number of qubits. This phenomenon, known specifically as a Noise-Induced Barren Plateau \(NIBP\), is conceptually distinct from noise-free plateaus because it flattens the entire landscape rather than just probabilistic regions. Because the gradient decays exponentially with circuit depth,)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
410 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 139 0 R
/E (To understand the deeper origin of these plateaus, researchers have developed a Lie algebraic theory that unifies different sources of information loss. The trainability of a circuit is inversely proportional to the dimension of its Dynamical Lie Algebra \(DLA\); more expressive circuits with larger DLAs lead to more concentrated loss functions \(Bansal et al., 2026\). Furthermore, the concept of g-purity measures the generalized entanglement of a state relative to this algebra. When noise is introduced, it creates "algebraic decoherence," building generalized entanglement or reducing the generalized locality of measurement operators. This framework proves that if a DLA has an exponential dimension, the circuit will inevitably lose its history and become untrainable, regardless of the specific initial state or measurement used. \(Ragone et al., 2024\))
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
411 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 139 0 R
/E (Lie Algebraic Unification )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
412 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 139 0 R
/E (of Information Loss)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
413 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 139 0 R
/E (The barren plateau phenomenon can be fundamentally viewed as a curse of dimensionality. When a quantum circuit avoids these plateaus, it is often because the computation has been restricted to a polynomially large subspace. While staying in these smaller subspaces makes the circuit trainable, it also potentially makes it classically simulable \(Singh et al., 2024 \))
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
414 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 139 0 R
/E (Restoring Signal Through)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
415 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 139 0 R
/E ( Engineered Dissipation)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
