423 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 140 0 R
/E (This creates a "soft dequantization" dilemma: many architectures provably free of barren plateaus can be efficiently surrogated by classical algorithms, provided some initial data is collected from a quantum device. Therefore, the structural traits that allow a circuit to retain its history and training signal are often the same traits that allow a classical computer to emulate the system's behavior, posing a challenge for finding a true quantum advantage. \(Cerezo et al., 2025\))
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
424 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 140 0 R
/T (REFERENCES)
/E (REFERENCES)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
425 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 140 0 R
/E (The tendency of deep quantum circuits to "forget" their history through noise-induced barren plateaus is a defining challenge for the NISQ era. This information loss is driven by the contractive nature of noise, the specific scaffolding of hardware connectivity, and the exponential dimension of operator algebras. However, by understanding these mechanisms, researchers are developing pathways toward resilience. Whether through the identification of classically simulable subspaces or the use of engineered dissipation to localize training signals, the focus is shifting toward architectures that balance expressiveness with physical trainability. Overcoming this crisis is the key to ensuring that quantum circuits can scale and ultimately deliver on their promise of computational transformation.)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
426 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 140 0 R
/E (Conclusion)
/Pg 24 0 R
/K [4]
>>
