/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 143 0 R
/E (storage. Unlike memory-based models, transformers do not store data directly but rather derive relations between tokens while performing inference [1].)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
472 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 143 0 R
/E (The self-attention mechanism allows each token in a sequence to focus on every other token in the same sequence, enabling the analysis of contextual dependencies and semantic relations [1][4]. This constitutes the underlying principle of memory simulation for LLMs as they can only make use of information contained within an input sequence.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
473 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 143 0 R
/E (Conversely, the self-attention mechanism entails some computational limitations due to quadratic growth in the number of computations performed during attention. This implies that the notion of memory in LLMs is closely associated with efficient and scalable attention mechanisms[6].)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
474 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 143 0 R
/E (Degradation of Contextual Attention)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
475 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 143 0 R
/E (With increasing input length, the capacity of the model to pay equal attention to each token decreases. This implies a degradation in remembering the initial part of the input.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
476 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 143 0 R
/E (No Built-In Persistence)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
477 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 143 0 R
/E (Inherently, large language models cannot remember information between sessions. They have to be reminded in each instance of their interaction.)
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
